- How would you adapt this for 128-bit using the same pattern?
- What are the performance implications on 32-bit vs 64-bit targets?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-boundary bit manipulation and 128-bit arithmetic, which are fundamental skills in cryptography and high-performance kernel development. It also evaluates the ability to implement rotation operations without branching logic.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across two 64-bit limbs
- Using a single 128-bit intermediate simplifies boundary handling
- Edge-case handling via rotation count masking (eliminates branches)

## Code Example

```cpp
void rotl128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  __uint128_t v = ((__uint128_t)hi << 64) | lo;
  k &= 127;
  __uint128_t r = (v << k) | (v >> (128 - k));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)r;
}
```

## Edge Cases

- `k = 0`: Returns original value unchanged
- `k = 64`: Swaps hi and lo parts completely

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend classic bit-trick patterns to wider word sizes without branches, a common need in performance-critical systems.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register)
- 128-bit data layout with two 64-bit halves
- Branchless bit counting and aggregation

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

int popcount128(uint128 a) {
  return (int)(popcount64(a.hi) + popcount64(a.lo));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for SIMD-enabled languages (e.g., intrinsics)?
- Compare branchless approach vs. lookup-based methods on large datasets.

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to implement conditional data movement without branching. It demonstrates how to construct a full-word mask from a single flag and apply it consistently across 128-bit halves.

## Key Concepts
- **Branchless conditioning via masks:** Using arithmetic to convert a boolean into an all-0s or all-1s mask
- **128-bit representation:** A 128-bit value split into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- **Portable unsigned arithmetic:** Safe, well-defined behavior across platforms

## Code Example
```c
uint128 swapHalvesIf128(uint128 x, unsigned c) {
    unsigned m = -(unsigned)(c != 0);
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi & ~m) | (x.lo & m);
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo & ~m) | (x.hi & m);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
}
```

